  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Computing Energy of Decaying Signals",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute total energy for decaying continuous-time signals by squaring the signal magnitude, setting the correct semi-infinite integration limits, and evaluating the resulting convergent improper integral.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Computing Energy of Decaying Signals",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Computing Energy of Decaying Signals",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Energy is still defined the same way for a decaying signal: integrate $|x(t)|^2$ over all time. The point is to keep the same definition while reading the support first, because a decaying signal usually lives on a semi-infinite interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|^2\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use $x(t)=e^{-at}u(t)$ with $a>0$ as the base case. The unit step makes the signal zero for $t<0$ and leaves the exponential tail on $[0,\\infty)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Once the support is known, square the magnitude and collapse the full-time integral to the tail on the signal's support. That is the only new move here: convert the whole-line energy integral into an improper integral over the nonzero part.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-2at}\\,dt=\\frac{1}{2a},\\qquad a>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The integral converges because $a>0$ makes the exponential decay on the support. If the exponent does not decay, the same energy setup does not produce a finite result.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The same pattern will keep working when the signal starts later or is scaled by a constant: find the support, square the magnitude, and integrate the tail from the correct lower limit.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E=\\int_{t_0}^\\infty |A|^2 e^{-2a(t-t_0)}\\,dt=\\frac{|A|^2}{2a},\\qquad a>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Computing Energy of Decaying Signals",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Energy of a One-Sided Decaying Exponential",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the total energy of $x(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the unit step: $u(t)$ makes $x(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$ zero for $t<0$, so the signal lives on $[0,\\infty)$. Energy still uses the same definition, so start with the full-time integral and then restrict it to the support.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|^2\\,dt=\\int_0^\\infty |e^{-t}|^2\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now square the magnitude. Since $e^{-t}$ is real-valued, $|e^{-t}|^2=e^{-2t}$, so the energy becomes a convergent improper integral on the one-sided support.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-2t}\\,dt=\\left[-\\frac{1}{2}e^{-2t}\\right]_0^\\infty=\\frac{1}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The result is finite because the exponential tail decays on its support. This is the pattern to reuse: read the support from the step function, square the magnitude, and integrate only the nonzero part.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Computing Energy of Decaying Signals",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Energy of a Scaled Decaying Exponential",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the total energy of $x(t)=3e^{-t}u(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is still the unit step: $u(t)$ makes $x(t)=3e^{-t}u(t)$ zero for $t<0$, so the signal lives on $[0,\\infty)$. The only new wrinkle is the amplitude $3$, which changes the magnitude but not the support.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|^2\\,dt=\\int_0^\\infty |3e^{-t}|^2\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Square the amplitude before integrating. Since $|3e^{-t}|^2=9e^{-2t}$, the energy becomes the same convergent improper integral as before, multiplied by the squared amplitude.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E=9\\int_0^\\infty e^{-2t}\\,dt=9\\left[-\\frac{1}{2}e^{-2t}\\right]_0^\\infty=\\frac{9}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The result is finite because the exponential tail still decays on its support. The lesson to reuse is simple: read the support from $u(t)$, square the whole magnitude, and let the amplitude factor carry through as a square.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Computing Energy of Decaying Signals",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Energy of a Decaying Exponential with a Nonzero Start Time",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the total energy of $x(t)=e^{-(t-3)}u(t-3)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the unit step $u(t-3)$, so the signal is zero for $t<3$. That means the energy integral starts at $3$, not at $0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|^2\\,dt=\\int_3^\\infty \\left|e^{-(t-3)}\\right|^2\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Squaring the magnitude keeps the same support and turns the integrand into $e^{-2(t-3)}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E=\\int_3^\\infty e^{-2(t-3)}\\,dt=\\left[-\\frac{1}{2}e^{-2(t-3)}\\right]_3^\\infty=\\frac{1}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The shift changes only the lower limit. The decay still makes the improper integral converge, so the total energy is $\\frac{1}{2}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Computing Energy of Decaying Signals",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Checking Convergence of a Decaying-Signal Energy Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For which values of $a$ does $x(t)=e^{-at}u(t)$ have finite energy, and what is the energy when it converges?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The unit step $u(t)$ fixes the support to $t\\ge 0$, so the energy test starts on $[0,\\infty)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|^2\\,dt=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-2at}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now inspect the exponent in $e^{-2at}$. If $a>0$, the integrand decays and the improper integral can converge. If $a=0$, the integrand stays at $1$. If $a<0$, it grows, so the energy diverges.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-2at}\\,dt=\\left[-\\frac{1}{2a}e^{-2at}\\right]_0^\\infty=\\frac{1}{2a}\\qquad (a>0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the convergence decision comes first: $x(t)$ has finite energy exactly when $a>0$, and then $E=\\frac{1}{2a}$. For $a\\le 0$, the energy is infinite.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
